WEBVTT
TITLE Mean, Median, Mode and Standard Deviation: Three Middles, One Spread, and When Each Rule Fails — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Nine salaries, one question: what does a person here earn? Three different answers, and all three are correct.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
The mean pools everything and shares it out: 312 over 9 gives 34.667.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
The median takes the fifth of nine sorted values: 30.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
The mode counts: 30 occurs three times, so the mode is 30.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Deviations from the mean: five negative, four positive, and they sum to zero.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
The mean is the balance point: one value at 90 balances eight small ones.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Squared deviations are smallest at the mean: 3526 here, against 3722 at the median.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Sweeping the reference point across a fine grid finds its minimum at 34.67 — the mean.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Sort first: the median is defined on the ordered list, not the list you were given.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Odd n: the middle value. Even n: the average of the two middle values.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Absolute deviations are smallest at the median: 82 here, against 110.67 at the mean.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
So: squared error → mean; absolute error → median. Two penalties, two answers.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Count how often each value occurs. 30 occurs three times: the highest count wins.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Two values can tie at the top: 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 4 is bimodal, with modes 1 and 2.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
And a list where every value occurs once has no mode at all.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
The mode is the only average that works on categories: a blood group has no mean.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Fifty observations in six classes of width 10. The raw values are gone.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Mean by midpoints: the weighted sum 1480 over 50 gives 29.6.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Median: the first class reaching N/2 = 25 is 20–30, giving 28.667.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Mode: the modal class 20–30 with neighbours 8 and 12 gives 27.0.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Spread asks how far the values sit from the mean — and the distances come in two signs.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Squaring removes the sign. The squared deviations total 3526.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Average the squares: variance 391.778. Take the root: standard deviation 19.793.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
The mean absolute deviation is 12.296 — not the standard deviation, and 38 per cent smaller.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Add 100 to every salary: every average moves by 100, the spread does not move.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Now multiply by 3: mean 204, median 190, mode 190, and the spread triples to 59.38.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Because y − mean(y) = a(x − mean(x)): the constant cancels, the factor survives.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
GATE CSE 2011 offered sigma_y = a·sigma_x + b as an option. That is the incorrect one.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Exponential with rate 0.5: mean 2, median 1.386, mode 0 — three different numbers.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Uniform: mean and median agree, but no unique mode. Normal: all three equal the mean.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Binomial with n = 1000, p = 0.4: mean 400, variance 240, standard deviation 15.49.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Y = max(X, 0) puts half the mass on zero, so the median is 0 while the mean is not.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
On the frequency table, mode 27.0 < median 28.667 < mean 29.6: the tail is on the right.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
The empirical relation mode ≈ 3·median − 2·mean gives 26.8 against the computed 27.0.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
The list 0, 1, 2, 3, 6, 6 has mean 3.0, median 2.5 and mode 6 — the ordering rule breaks.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
So use the ordering to sanity-check an answer, never to derive one.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
First question: is the wording about mass, position or frequency?

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Second question: a list or a distribution? A raw table or a grouped one?

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Four numbers from this lesson: mean 34.667, median 30, mode 30, sigma 19.793.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Decide what the question is measuring before you compute anything.

